# spec: Conversational Router and Pair Programmer

Use this skill for `/spec <intent>`, any development task, feature request, troubleshooting or general engineering question. It routes each intent to the delivery workflow and skill that fit.

## Constraints

- Run `devkit spec policy` before classifying work. If it fails, stop policy-dependent work.
- Explicit user instructions and authorization already given take precedence.
- Before deciding an approval boundary or a Git action, read the [completion policy](references/completion.md#policy).
- Do not declare the user's session finished or stop active tasks without the user's direction.
- Read each reference at the condition its link names; do not load every template, instruction or playbook up front.
- Do not search or route to playbooks from `spec`. A playbook runs only when the user invokes `devkit-playbook` for the task or a failing rule's remediation names one; handle any other operational request as ordinary work.
- Under `FEEDBACK_POLICY reminder=contextual`, suggest an optional `/spec-feedback` sweep when completed work produced reusable observations; it never gates completion or merge. Routine completions, proposal additions and wording edits get no reminder. Under `reminder=off`, give none.

## Route the intent

1. Classify on the three axes of the spec constitution (`devkit get-context spec`), in order: `task-kind`, `planning-depth`, then per activity the mode and `activity-effort` ([effort](references/effort.md)). For Backlog, inline Planning, depth changes and closes, follow [classification](references/classification.md).
2. State the classification and activity effort, then proceed within the authorized intent. Ask only when the goal, scope or constraints are unresolved.
3. Before creating a Unit or making the first edit, read [git policy](references/git-policy.md) and settle the branch: in a new worktree per [worktree](references/worktree.md); without a worktree, or on an existing branch, per [branches](references/branches.md).
4. Run the workflow of the classification:

| Classification | Workflow | Planning and start |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `session` × `feature` | `sdd-session-feature` | Inline Planning: agree the intent in the conversation; no records. Implement, verify and review at the effort, following `devkit workflow show sdd-session-feature`. |
| `session` × `bugfix` | `sdd-session-bugfix` | Same, with the expected behavior agreed; a failing reproduction test first (Red), then the fix (Green), reporting both. |
| `overall-refactor` (no depth) | `sdd-overall-refactor` | `direct` only, with the user, never with the predefined workers; follow [classification](references/classification.md) §5. |
| `spec` × `feature` | `sdd-spec-feature` | Inline Planning on `dev-<slug>`: `devkit spec new <slug> --title "..." --summary "..."`, record `plan-intent`, commit. Development runs `spec-specify` ➔ `spec-plan` ➔ `spec-tasks` and the later stages. |
| `spec` × `bugfix` | `sdd-spec-bugfix` | Same with `--workflow sdd-spec-bugfix`. Development characterizes the defect (`spec-specify`, bugfix reference), reproduces with a `red` envelope and verifies with a `green` one. |
| `proposal` (either kind) | `sdd-proposal`, then `sdd-spec-<task_kind>` | Backlog: `devkit spec proposal new <slug> --task-kind feature\|bugfix [--planning-effort ...] [--dev-effort ...]`, recording the authorized intent as `plan-intent`. Planning runs on `plan-<slug>` to `plan-accept` per [planning](references/planning.md), in `direct` mode or by a `planning-worker`; a direct acceptance in Backlog skips it ([backlog](references/backlog.md)). Development starts on `dev-<slug>` with `devkit spec proposal handoff <slug>` and the `spec-next` skill's post-handoff link check. |
| Spike, independent review, or reconcile verification in an isolated context | Support role | `spec-delegate` (`scout`, `challenger`, `code-reviewer`, `reconcile-reviewer`), as the `activity-effort` asks. |
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}| Native approval preparation, or integration after CLI/PR `dev-integration` authorization | Integration | `spec-integrate`: base integration, re-verify changed parts, confirm reconcile, final checks. |
{% else %}| Integration after separately existing CLI/PR `dev-integration` authorization | Integration | `spec-integrate`: base integration, re-verify changed parts, confirm reconcile, final checks. |
{% endif %}
Run `devkit workflow show --unit <dir>` (or `<workflow-id>` before a Unit exists) to see a workflow's stages, checks, gates, `exception` stage and returns.

## Approval boundaries

- The three boundaries are `plan-accept` (proposal direction), `proposal-handoff` (start Development) and `dev-integration` (land the verified result). Each follows its own `ask|auto` from `devkit spec policy`, as the [completion policy](references/completion.md#policy) defines.
- Under `auto`, cite the policy as the authority; never fabricate a user answer.
- `plan-intent` records the task intent, not a fourth approval; an existing explicit task instruction satisfies it without another confirmation. Record actual user intent with `devkit workflow answer --unit <dir> --request gate:plan-intent --action accept --by user`.
- Record a direction acceptance with `devkit spec proposal accept`, which also writes the status.
- Pass `--given` to assert a runner check (`lint-ci-clean`, `merged`) per run.
- Development has no direction gate: implementation, decomposition, review and refinement within the agreement are agent decisions ([classification](references/classification.md) §2).

## Binding, returns and records

- `devkit spec new` and proposal handoff bind the Unit. Bind one made otherwise with `devkit workflow bind --unit <dir> --workflow <id>`; definition changes apply without rebinding.
- To go back within Development, run `devkit workflow return --unit <dir> --to <stage>`; it voids later approvals and envelopes. Development never returns to Planning; a direction change is a close ([classification](references/classification.md) §4).
- Envelopes live under the Unit's `envelopes/` ([envelope](references/envelope.md)). Record a run's questions, problems, answers and resolutions per [worker records](references/worker-records.md).

## Completion

1. When approaching review or completion, read [completion](references/completion.md).
2. Before `dev-integration`, run the `collect-feedback` sweep the workflow names (`spec-feedback`; skipped under `[STAGE_POLICY: collect-feedback state=off]`).
3. Present the final change report in the [envelope](references/envelope.md) format.
4. Integrate with `spec-integrate` under the [completion policy](references/completion.md#policy).

- Commit with plain `git commit`; the commit gate runs the staged checks.
- Before a Planning run returns, follow [per-run feedback](../spec-feedback/references/sweep.md#planning-run-coverage).
- Before deleting a proposal without handoff, follow [feedback retirement](references/feedback-retirement.md).

The task is done when the final change report is presented and code review, commit and integration are each reported as a separate fact with its authority.
